{"name":"Beryl Red","hex":"#a16381","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beryl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral of pegmatite deposits, often used as a gemstone (molecular formula Be3Al2Si6O18).","An example (a stone) of the mineral beryl."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dull bluish green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beryl"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beryl","blocks":[{"text":"Beryl ( BERR-əl) is a mineral composed of beryllium aluminium silicate with the chemical formula Be3Al2(SiO3)6. Well-known varieties of beryl include emerald and aquamarine. Naturally occurring hexagonal crystals of beryl can be up to several meters in size, but terminated crystals are relatively rare. Pure beryl is colorless, but it is frequently tinted by impurities; possible colors are green, blue, yellow, pink, and red (the rarest). It is an ore source of beryllium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Beryl09.jpg/330px-Beryl09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beryl","wikidata":"Q103480"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/500px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/1280px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/500px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Main beryl producing countries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AB%C3%A9ryl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png/500px-Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png/1280px-Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png/500px-Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beryl crystal structure with view down C axis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeryl_Crystal_Structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Beryl-209736.jpg/500px-Beryl-209736.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut